[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 968,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 733,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136899,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 7,
        "headingId": 733,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Reactive epithelial hyperplasia is a reactive, benign, non-neoplastic epithelial proliferation, in response to an irritating stimulus.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 969,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 734,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136900,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 7,
        "headingId": 734,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 970,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 735,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136901,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 7,
        "headingId": 735,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>9A6Y Other specified disorders of conjunctiva</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 971,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 736,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136902,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 7,
        "headingId": 736,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> pseudoepitheliomatous hyperplasia; pseudoadenomatous hyperplasia.</p>\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> pseudocarcinomatous hyperplasia.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 972,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 737,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136903,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 7,
        "headingId": 737,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 973,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 738,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136904,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 7,
        "headingId": 738,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Pseudoepitheliomatous hyperplasia tends to affect (limbal) bulbar conjunctiva. Pseudoglandular hyperplasia preferentially occurs in the fornix and tarsal conjunctiva.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 974,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 739,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136905,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 7,
        "headingId": 739,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Pseudoepitheliomatous hyperplasia may appear as a fleshy to white mass, minimally raised or thickened {17181623}. Pseudoglandular hyperplasia appears as a fleshy, poorly defined mass {11150278} and shares clinical features with ocular surface neoplasia.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 975,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 740,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136906,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 7,
        "headingId": 740,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Lesions are uncommon; they have been reported in children and adults of both sexes.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 976,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 741,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136907,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 7,
        "headingId": 741,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>In many cases there is no known association. These lesions have been associated with granular cell tumour, blastomycosis, thermal burns, epithelial (limbal) cell transplantation, contact lens use, chemical exposure (e.g. iododerma), and previous surgery {11150278;3992475;6258438;17181623;7322470;6888855}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 977,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 742,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136908,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 7,
        "headingId": 742,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Lesions arise as a result of nonspecific reactive changes in response to diverse presumed causal associations.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 984,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 749,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136909,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 7,
        "headingId": 749,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>See <em>Clinical features</em>, above.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 978,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 743,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136910,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 7,
        "headingId": 743,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Pseudoepitheliomatous hyperplasia consists of acanthotic epithelium with normal keratinocyte maturation. Squamous metaplasia may be present with surface keratinization, and the superficial epithelial cells can contain keratohyaline granules. Pseudoglandular hyperplasia is composed of prominent pseudoglands of Henle, lined with stratified squamous epithelium with goblet cells. Mucin often fills the lumina of the pseudoglands, and lamellar concretions are sometimes noted.</p>\r\n<p>The histological differential diagnosis for pseudoepitheliomatous hyperplasia includes papilloma, inverted papilloma, and conjunctival squamous intraepithelial neoplasia. Normal maturation of keratinocytes is lost in epithelial dysplasia, and keratinocytes display pleomorphic nuclei with variable amounts of cytoplasm and mitotic activity.</p>\r\n<p>The histological differential diagnosis for pseudoglandular hyperplasia is squamous cell carcinoma with mucinous differentiation (previously known as mucoepidermoid carcinoma). This will be more cellular and the keratinocytes will display nuclear atypia with focal intracytoplasmic mucin and mitotic activity.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 979,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 744,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136911,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 7,
        "headingId": 744,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Smears show benign squamous epithelium.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 980,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 745,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136912,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 7,
        "headingId": 745,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>There are no known genetic abnormalities.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 983,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 748,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136913,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 7,
        "headingId": 748,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> hyperplastic epithelium with benign cytology.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> identification of an underlying causal association.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 981,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 746,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136914,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 7,
        "headingId": 746,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not applicable</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 982,
    "bookid": 65,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 747,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 136915,
        "bookId": 65,
        "chapterId": 7,
        "headingId": 747,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These are benign/reactive processes with a good prognosis. There are no reports about recurrences after incomplete removal or eradication of the underlying cause.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2026-03-31T14:57:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]